# The build context is copied wholesale by `COPY . .` in the Dockerfile.
# Anything listed here is either rebuilt inside the image, secret, or actively
# harmful to carry in.

# Rebuilt by `npm ci` in the deps stage.
node_modules

# The killer: a local .next left behind by `next dev` (webpack or turbopack)
# lands in the image and `next build` then runs on top of a manifest that
# describes a different router. That is what surfaces as
# "Cannot find module for page: /_document" during page data collection.
.next
out
build

# Local state that must never be baked into an image.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
media
# Left over from the SQLite era. Still listed because a developer who has been
# on this repository since then still has these files locally, and a stale
# database.db in the context would be copied into the image and quietly
# ignored — a 40MB layer nobody can explain.
database.db
database.db-*
# A content dump is somebody's export of the whole CMS, reservations and
# subscriber addresses included. It has no business in an image.
content-export.json

# Housekeeping
.git
.gitignore
.github
.claude
*.tsbuildinfo
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
.DS_Store
Dockerfile
docker-compose.yml
README.md
DEPLOY.md
# The database and backup images are built from their own contexts
# (ops/postgres, ops/pgbackrest); none of that belongs in the application
# image. The exceptions are the two scripts the application container runs
# around the server — the negations have to come after the exclusion, or Docker
# never reconsiders the directory.
ops
!ops/preflight.mjs
!ops/warm-up.sh
